e,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_074","cnm_p121"],"id":"solution:repeated-pythagoras:7fbe1da4cae9","label":"Find the height using the Pythagorean theorem","methodDirectNodeIds":{"repeated-pythagoras":["cnm_174","cnm_p089","cnm_169"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_p089","cnm_169","cnm_186","cnm_074","cnm_p121"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 3/4","reason":"The sixth root of 27 is sqrt(3), while sqrt(6 3/4) = 3sqrt(3)/2. Their difference is -sqrt(3)/2, whose square is 3/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1986_AMC_12_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_168","cnm_169","cnm_170","cnm_172"],"id":"1986_AMC_12_Q05_m1","label":"Simplify the roots before squaring","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1986_AMC_12_Q05_m1":["cnm_173","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_173","cnm_h039","cnm_168","cnm_169","cnm_170","cnm_172"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2√3","reason":"If the side is s, the altitude bisects the base, so s² = (s/2)² + 6. Thus s² = 8 and s = 2√2. The area is (2√2)(√6)/2 = 2√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_112","cnm_075","cnm_169"],"id":"altitude-and-pythagoras","label":"Find the side using the altitude","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-and-pythagoras":["cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_139","cnm_112","cnm_075","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 6√6 inches","reason":"The smaller triangle has 2/3 of the original area. Thus its side parallel to the side of length 18 is 18√(2/3)=6√6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_p148","cnm_075","cnm_169"],"id":"similar-triangle-area-ratio","label":"Use the remaining triangle's area","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-triangle-area-ratio":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_220","cnm_069","cnm_p148","cnm_075","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 1","reason":"The square of the numerator of the fraction is 2sqrt(5)+2, so the positive fraction equals sqrt(2). Also sqrt(3-2sqrt(2))=sqrt(2)-1. Their difference is 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_168","cnm_148","cnm_169","cnm_167"],"id":"square-the-positive-fraction","label":"Simplify by squaring and recognizing a perfect square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-the-positive-fraction":["cnm_173","cnm_147","cnm_153"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_173","cnm_147","cnm_153","cnm_168","cnm_148","cnm_169","cnm_167"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 23","reason":"Name the left and right interior points F and G. Rhombi ABCG and AEDF have sides 2 and diagonals BG = EF = 2, giving AC = AD = 2√3. Triangles ABC and ADE each have area √3. Triangle ACD has base 2 and height √11, so the total area is 2√3+√11 = √12+√11. Hence m+n = 23.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_169"],"id":"solution:rhombus-diagonals-and-altitudes:0824ce71efe8","label":"Rhombus diagonals and triangle heights","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rhombus-diagonals-and-altitudes":["cnm_p150","cnm_190","cnm_189","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_190","cnm_189","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 3","reason":"Let R be a semicircle radius. Adjacent centers give R²+(1-R)²=(2R)², so R=(√3-1)/2. The square of centers has side 2R, so the small radius is (√2-1)R. Its diameter is therefore (√3-1)(√2-1), giving a+b+c+d=3-1+2-1=3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_245","cnm_250","cnm_169"],"id":"tangent-centers-and-right-triangles","label":"Connect the centers of tangent circles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-centers-and-right-triangles":["cnm_h181","cnm_174","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_174","cnm_171","cnm_191","cnm_245","cnm_250","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: b=+4sqrt(2) or -4sqrt(2), both irrational","reason":"Completing the square gives y=-(x-b/2)^2+b^2/4-8. Zero vertex height requires b^2=32, so b=+/-4sqrt(2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","cnm_231","cnm_075","cnm_169"],"id":"solution:zero-vertex-height:7a67b01b644b","label":"Find the vertex by completing the square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"zero-vertex-height":["cnm_232","cnm_220","cnm_077"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_232","cnm_220","cnm_077","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_231","cnm_075","cnm_169"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_219","cnm_075","cnm_169"],"id":"solution:double-root-condition:7a67b01b644b","label":"Use the repeated-root condition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"double-root-condition":["cnm_234","cnm_223","cnm_220","cnm_077"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_234","cnm_223","cnm_220","cnm_077","cnm_219","cnm_075","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 9sqrt(2)/2 inches","reason":"If the triangle side is t, its area is sqrt(3)t^2/4=9sqrt(3), so t=6 and its perimeter is 18. The square side is 18/4=9/2, giving diagonal 9sqrt(2)/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_p122","cnm_p077","cnm_075","cnm_169"],"id":"altitude-area-perimeter","label":"Find the triangle side, then the square diagonal","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-area-perimeter":["cnm_p148","cnm_p078","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_p078","cnm_174","cnm_137","cnm_p122","cnm_p077","cnm_075","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 2√22","reason":"AC=16. Power at A gives AH(AH+7)=2·15=30, so AH=3, BJ=6, and BH=13. Put x=BD and y=BE. Powers at B and C give xy=78 and (16-x)(16-y)=14, hence x+y=20. Thus DE²=(y-x)²=20²-4·78=88, so DE=2√22.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1982_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_146","cnm_169"],"id":"secant-products","label":"Compare secant products at the vertices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"secant-products":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_224","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:power-of-a-point","cnm_224","cnm_148","cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_146","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (2sqrt(3))","reason":"The common domain is [6,8]. There, 8x-x^2=16-(x-4)^2 is at most 12, and the second radical is nonnegative. Thus f(x) is at most sqrt(12), with equality at x=6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_148","cnm_169"],"id":"solution:quadratic-upper-bound:f51e51d0c7ab","label":"Find the domain and an attained upper bound","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-upper-bound":["cnm_195","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235","cnm_079"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_195","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235","cnm_079","cnm_167","cnm_148","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: √6 - √2","reason":"Take the tangent line as y = 0 and P = (0,1). Since PQ = 3 and QR = 5, their horizontal separations are √(9-1) = 2√2 and √(25-1) = 2√6. Hence Q = (2√2,2) and R = (2√2+2√6,3). The area is one half of |(2√2)(2) - (2√2+2√6)(1)|, which equals √6 - √2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_260","cnm_110","cnm_169"],"id":"horizontal-midpoint-base","label":"A horizontal base through the midpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"horizontal-midpoint-base":["cnm_h181","cnm_p148","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_p148","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_185","cnm_260","cnm_110","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 10∜8","reason":"Let r = AB/H. The upright area-36 and area-4 rectangles have widths 6/√r and 2/√r. Together they span the area-32 rectangle, whose width is √(32r). Thus 8/√r = √(32r), giving r = √2. The areas sum to 200, so AB = √(200√2) = 10∜8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_268","cnm_169","cnm_170"],"id":"2024_AMC_10A_Q16_m1","label":"Find the common side ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"2024_AMC_10A_Q16_m1":["cnm_270","cnm_p089","cnm_p150","cnm_220","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_270","cnm_p089","cnm_p150","cnm_220","cnm_h039","cnm_268","cnm_169","cnm_170"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 1250√3 square miles","reason":"The actual short diagonal is (3/16)(400/(3/2))=50 miles. A rhombus diagonal opposite a 60-degree angle equals its side, so its area is 2(50²√3/4)=1250√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q37","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p185","cnm_112","cnm_075","cnm_169"],"id":"convert-length-and-split-rhombus","label":"Convert the diagonal and find two triangle areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"convert-length-and-split-rhombus":["cnm_p212","cnm_189","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p212","cnm_189","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_p185","cnm_112","cnm_075","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 14√5","reason":"DB=DC=7, so ∠DBC=∠BCD. Since BD bisects ∠ABC, triangles ABD and ACB are similar. Hence AB^2=AD·AC=9·16=144. Triangle ABD has sides 7,9,12 and semiperimeter 14, so its area is √(14·7·5·2)=14√5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_271","cnm_114","cnm_082","cnm_169"],"id":"similarity-and-altitude","label":"Find a side by similarity, then find an altitude","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similarity-and-altitude":["cnm_133","cnm_272","cnm_174","cnm_042","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_133","cnm_272","cnm_174","cnm_042","cnm_p148","cnm_137","cnm_271","cnm_114","cnm_082","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":10,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c92a5630f9f8bf6c54c9ee7c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 10π + 4√3","reason":"External tangency makes the center triangle's side 4 and its area 4√3. The three sectors total 3(60/360)π·2² = 2π. Thus the shaded area is 12π + 4√3 - 2π = 10π + 4√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_110","cnm_075","cnm_169"],"id":"2012_AMC_10B_Q16_m1","label":"Find the central gap","methodDirectNodeIds":{"2012_AMC_10B_Q16_m1":["cnm_257","cnm_p192","cnm_h181","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_257","cnm_p192","cnm_h181","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_250","cnm_110","cnm_075","cnm_169"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_169"],"label":"二次根式的乘法","layer":"CN","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"criteria":["能进行二次根式乘法","能逆用法则化简"],"description":"√a·√b=√(ab)（a≥0，b≥0），可逆用化简。","id":"cnm_169","label":"二次根式的乘法","orig